<Base title="Research | Waking Matter" description="How should prior experience change an agent’s next decision, and when should inherited knowledge be forgotten, reset or distrusted?" canonical="https://wakingmatter.com/research/" current="research">
  <main id="content" class="wrap">
    <header class="page-hero"><p class="label">Research</p><h1>How should experience change the next action?</h1><p class="lede">The question connects memory to control: how should prior experience change the next allocation of retrieval, attention, compute and action? These are questions we are investigating, with improvement to be demonstrated.</p></header>
    <div class="prose-page"><p class="label">Current questions</p><div class="inquiry">
      <article><p class="meta">01 / Experience into action</p><h2>What should the agent do differently?</h2><p>A useful experience should affect a decision: what to retrieve, which source to trust, what to test, which branch to pursue, when to stop and when to escalate. How can those changes be explicit enough to evaluate?</p></article>
      <article><p class="meta">02 / Memory into useful state</p><h2>What deserves to persist?</h2><p>Which facts, procedures and failed attempts are worth retaining? In what representation, at what scope, and for how long? Provenance should let a later agent inspect where a claim came from and what would invalidate it.</p></article>
      <article><p class="meta">03 / Changing worlds</p><h2>When should the past lose authority?</h2><p>Prior knowledge can become stale, misleading or harmful. How should a system detect that change, revise its state, distrust an inherited rule, or reset? What should remain local for reasons of privacy or context?</p></article>
      <article><p class="meta">04 / Evaluation into improvement</p><h2>Did experience actually help?</h2><p>Compare retained experience with a fresh start, with extra context or compute, and with advice available before the experience. Check outcomes, cost, repeated errors and escalation under changing conditions. A useful experiment must be able to show help, no effect or harm.</p></article>
    </div></div>
    <div class="prose-page split"><p class="label">A starting point</p><div class="measure">
      <p><a href="https://github.com/avs-io/agent-memory-contracts">Agent Memory Contracts</a> makes source evidence, candidate claims, promotion decisions and trusted records explicit through Python contracts, JSON schemas and validation. Its reference runtime and tests are publicly inspectable.</p>
      <p>Memory integrity is a substrate for this work. It does not by itself demonstrate learning from experience.</p>
      <p>These questions sit alongside continual and lifelong learning, retrieval and external memory, replay, distillation, adaptive control and cumulative culture. The longer thesis is in <a href="/notes/intelligence-should-accumulate/">Intelligence should accumulate</a>.</p>
    </div></div>
  </main>
</Base>
